08}, "rating_margin": 4.3, "sigma_race": 6.6, "sigma_total": 7.24, "baseline_margin": 5.53, "analyst_adjustment": -1.03, "key_factors": ["Expert raters lean Democratic overall: five call it Lean D, three Tossup, two Likely D", "Prediction markets price Moskowitz at roughly 70-73 percent", "Moskowitz holds a large cash-on-hand edge while Singer leans on outside groups and Chamber backing", "R+3 district offset by a favorable midterm environment; only stale May polls are available"], "rationale": "Ratings and betting markets both point to a modest Moskowitz edge, and his fundraising advantage plus a midterm climate tilted toward Democrats offsets the district's Republican lean. With no fresh polling, uncertainty remains wide.", "watch": "Fresh public polling, a surge of super PAC spending on either side, or shifts in voters' views of Trump could push the race to Tossup or solidify the incumbent's edge.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Jared Moskowitz (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Democratic incumbent Jared Moskowitz seeks reelection in a South Florida district that leans Republican by three points. He faces Republican Scott Singer, backed by the U.S. Chamber of Commerce and former Democratic Congressman Peter Deutsch, plus a minor Libertarian candidate. Expert raters mostly call the race Lean Democratic, though three of ten rate it a Tossup. Betting markets give Moskowitz roughly a seventy percent chance of winning.\n\nMoskowitz holds a clear cash advantage, about $1.9 million on hand to Singer's $476,000, while Singer draws more outside-group support and has launched the first television ads of the fall. Sparse May polling showed Moskowitz ahead by double digits, but no fresh public surveys are available. The race is expected to hinge on midterm turnout, cost-of-living politics, and voters' views of Trump.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7798, "mar